\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig_pipeline_pb.png}
\caption{ContamCal pipeline. Stage~1: controlled SFT injection creates 30 checkpoints across six families and five dosage levels (\S\ref{sec:injection}). Stage~2: multi-benchmark evaluation and dose-response classification (\S\ref{sec:classification}). Stage~3: RRF signal fusion (\S\ref{sec:stage1}) feeds contamination-augmented 2PL+$\gamma$ IRT (\S\ref{sec:stage2}) to produce decontaminated ability estimates and domain-level diagnostics.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

ContamCal operates in two stages: Stage~1 estimates per-item contamination exposure from multiple detection signals via training-free fusion; Stage~2 embeds these scores in a contamination-augmented IRT model to produce calibrated ability estimates with credible intervals (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}

Let $y_{ijk} \in \{0,1\}$ denote the binary response of model~$j$ ($j=1,\ldots,J$) to item~$i$ ($i=1,\ldots,I_k$) on benchmark~$k$. Standard IRT assumes $P(y_{ijk}=1) = f(\theta_j, \boldsymbol{\xi}_i)$ where $\theta_j$ is latent ability and $\boldsymbol{\xi}_i$ collects item parameters. When training corpora contain benchmark items, the observed ability $\hat{\theta}_j$ confounds genuine capability with memorization.

Our goal is to estimate $\theta_j^{\text{clean}}$, the ability each model would exhibit absent contamination, and to diagnose contamination effects at domain granularity. We adapt item compromise IRT \citep{eckerly_2017_item_compromise,sinharay_2017_preknowledge} and explanatory IRT \citep{deboeck_2004_explanatory_irt} for LLM evaluation: replacing binary leakage indicators with continuous exposure scores $e_{ij} \in [0,1]$, fitting per-benchmark models to handle the shared item pool \citep{sanmartin_2009_irt}, and recovering clean ability via counterfactual inference (setting $e_{ij}=0$).

\subsection{Stage 1: Multi-Signal Exposure Mapping}
\label{sec:stage1}

For each model~$j$ and item~$i$, we consider four candidate detection signals. \textbf{S1}~(Embedding similarity): cosine similarity between the item embedding and its nearest neighbor in the SFT training corpus. \textbf{S2}~(Min-K\%++): a membership inference statistic based on token-level surprisal \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp}. \textbf{S3}~(ConStat): the accuracy gap between original and paraphrased item variants \citep{dekoninck2024constat}. \textbf{S4}~(Self-Critique): negative entropy of model self-evaluation distributions, inspired by the finding that LLMs can assess their own knowledge \citep{kadavath2022}; concurrent work by \citet{tao2025detecting} uses a related but mechanistically distinct entropy collapse approach targeting RL-trained models. S3 requires paired paraphrased items as reference and is therefore limited to benchmarks with existing paraphrase sets. Our primary pipeline uses the remaining three signals ($\mathcal{S} = \{\text{S1, S2, S4}\}$) and applies to any benchmark without auxiliary data. S2 and S4 exhibit known failure modes under RL post-training, which we characterize in \S\ref{sec:signal_failure}. Item-level classification performance of the exposure scores is reported in Appendix~\ref{sec:sij_classification}.

We aggregate signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion \citep[RRF;][]{cormack_2009_rrf}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rrf}
    e_{ij} = \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}} \frac{1}{\kappa + r_{s}(i)},
\end{equation}
where $r_s(i)$ is the rank of item~$i$ under signal~$s$ (rank~1 = most suspicious), $\kappa=60$ is the standard RRF smoothing constant, robust to near-random component signals, and $|\mathcal{S}|=3$ in our primary configuration. Raw scores are min-max normalized per model to $e_{ij} \in [0,1]$; the distribution is highly right-skewed (mean 0.041).

\subsection{Stage 2: Contamination-Augmented IRT}
\label{sec:stage2}

\paragraph{Primary model: 2PL+$\gamma$.}
Our primary specification augments the two-parameter logistic (2PL) model \citep{lord_1980_irt,hambleton_1991_irt} with a contamination covariate. We fit independent IRT models per benchmark~$k$, each with its own contamination effect $\gamma_k$. Per-benchmark fitting avoids cross-benchmark item parameter borrowing while permitting heterogeneous contamination effects. For benchmark~$k$, the response probability is:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:2pl_gamma}
    P(y_{ijk} = 1) = \sigma\!\bigl(a_i(\theta_j - b_i) + \gamma_k \cdot e_{ij}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(\cdot)$ is the logistic function, $a_i > 0$ is item discrimination, $b_i \in \mathbb{R}$ is item difficulty, $\theta_j$ is model ability, and $\gamma_k$ is the scalar contamination effect for benchmark~$k$. Each benchmark is fitted independently; when finer-grained diagnosis is needed, we replace $\gamma_k$ with per-domain effects $\gamma_d$ ($d=1,\ldots,D$), fitting Eq.~\eqref{eq:2pl_gamma} separately for each cognitive domain within a benchmark (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}).

We place $\gamma_k$ outside the item discrimination bracket, modeling contamination as a uniform logit shift \citep{de_ayala_2009_irt} rather than the non-uniform DIF form that would scale contamination effects with item discrimination. For MC-format benchmarks, a 4PL extension adds guessing and ceiling asymptotes (Appendix~\ref{sec:4pl_details}); this requires $J \geq 16$ and is not used in domain-level analyses ($J = 13$).

\paragraph{Inference.}
We perform Bayesian inference via NUTS \citep{hoffman2014} in Stan \citep{carpenter2017} with four chains and target acceptance 0.95. Priors: $\theta_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$, $a_i \sim \text{LogNormal}(0, 0.5)$, $b_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$, $\gamma_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$. Convergence requires split-$\hat{R} < 1.01$ and bulk ESS~$> 400$ for all parameters. Model fit is assessed via the posterior predictive $p$-value (PPP), where values near 0.5 indicate adequate fit.

\paragraph{Calibrated score.}
The decontaminated accuracy for model~$j$ on benchmark~$k$ is the posterior expectation under zero exposure:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:calibrated}
    \text{Acc}_{j}^{\text{cal}} = \frac{1}{I_k} \sum_{i=1}^{I_k}\, \mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\psi} \sim p(\boldsymbol{\psi} \mid \mathbf{y})} \!\left[\sigma\!\bigl(a_i(\theta_j - b_i)\bigr)\right],
\end{equation}
with credible intervals from posterior quantiles. In practice, we average $\sigma(a_i(\theta_j - b_i))$ over posterior draws with $e_{ij}=0$; the spread of draws yields the credible interval. Models with noisier exposure signals receive wider intervals by construction.

\paragraph{Identifiability.}
\label{sec:fisher}
\label{sec:value_decomposition}
The reliability of $\gamma$ depends on signal AUC and ensemble size $J$; simulation validates that recovery requires AUC$\geq$0.55 with $J \geq 50$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:identifiability_appendix}). Empirically, exposure scores $e_{ij}$ and item difficulty $b_i$ are near-orthogonal (Pearson $|r|<0.13$, $R^2<0.014$ across benchmarks), confirming that $\gamma$ is identified from dosage-induced variation rather than confounded with difficulty. Eq.~\eqref{eq:calibrated} depends on $\theta_j$, not $\gamma$ directly, predicting that vanilla 2PL ($\gamma\!=\!0$) achieves comparable calibration to 2PL+$\gamma$, with $\gamma_k$ serving diagnosis rather than calibration (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}).

\subsection{Dose-Response Profile Classification}
\label{sec:classification}

Independent of the IRT calibration pipeline, we formalize how each model's accuracy trajectory across dosage levels is classified into a canonical dose-response pattern, drawing on the dose-response modeling tradition in pharmacology \citep{hill1910aggregation,ritz2015dose}. Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification} defines a deterministic decision tree over the accuracy vector $\mathbf{a} = (a_0, a_5, a_{25}, a_{50}, a_{100})$, parameterized by noise threshold $\tau$, severity threshold $\sigma$, saturation ratio $\rho$, and late-gain floor $\lambda$. The top-level split is governed by $\Delta = a_{100} - a_0$ relative to $\tau$; within-branch refinements distinguish Collapse from V-recovery via mid-dosage trough detection. A mid-trajectory volatility check ($v^*$) distinguishes true immunity (Phi-4: $v^* = 0.34$pp) from V-recovery with full endpoint recovery (Qwen-7B seed$=$123: $v^* = 3.22$pp $> \tau$). The default $\tau=2$\,pp corresponds to approximately one standard deviation of cross-seed accuracy variability. All thresholds are explicit and adjustable; a 30-combination grid sweep confirms all families are stable across all $(\tau, \sigma)$ combinations (180/180 model--seed--threshold pairs; Appendix~\ref{sec:classification_sensitivity}).

\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\caption{Dose-Response Profile Classification}
\label{alg:profile-classification}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Accuracy vector $\mathbf{a} = (a_{0}, a_{5}, a_{25}, a_{50}, a_{100})$ at dosages $d \in \{0, 5, 25, 50, 100\}\%$
\Require Noise threshold $\tau = 2$ pp, severity threshold $\sigma = 5$ pp
\Require Saturation ratio $\rho = 0.3$, late-gain floor $\lambda = 0.5$ pp
\Ensure Pattern $p \in \{$\textsc{Immune}, \textsc{Collapse},
\Statex \hspace{\algorithmicindent}\textsc{V-recovery}, \textsc{Benefit}$\}$
\State $\Delta \gets a_{100} - a_{0}$ \Comment{end-to-end accuracy shift}
\State $m^* \gets \min(a_{5},\, a_{25},\, a_{50})$ \Comment{mid-dosage minimum}
\State $v^* \gets \max_{i \in \{5,25,50\}} |a_i - a_0|$ \Comment{max mid-trajectory deviation}
\If{$|\Delta| \le \tau$}
    \If{$v^* \le \tau$}
        \State \Return \textsc{Immune} \Comment{fluctuation within noise band}
    \ElsIf{$m^* < a_{0}$ \textbf{and} $m^* < a_{100}$}
        \State \Return \textsc{V-recovery} \Comment{V-dip with full endpoint recovery}
    \Else
        \State \Return \textsc{Immune} \Comment{non-V volatility, endpoint stable}
    \EndIf
\ElsIf{$\Delta < -\tau$}
    \If{$\Delta \le -\sigma$}
        \State \Return \textsc{Collapse} \Comment{severe monotonic degradation}
    \ElsIf{$m^* < a_{0}$ \textbf{and} $m^* < a_{100}$}
        \State \Return \textsc{V-recovery} \Comment{mid-dosage trough with partial recovery}
    \Else
        \State \Return \textsc{Collapse} \Comment{moderate decline, no V-shape}
    \EndIf
\Else \Comment{$\Delta > \tau$}
    \State $g_1 \gets a_{25} - a_{0}$ \Comment{first-half gain}
    \State $g_2 \gets a_{100} - a_{25}$ \Comment{second-half gain}
    \State $\ell \gets a_{100} - a_{50}$ \Comment{late gain}
    \If{$(g_1 > 0$ \textbf{and} $g_2 < \rho \cdot g_1)$ \textbf{or} $\ell < \lambda$}
        \State \Return \textsc{Benefit} \Comment{saturating gain}
    \Else
        \State \Return \textsc{Benefit} \Comment{sustained gain}
    \EndIf
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\paragraph{Family-level aggregation.}
Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification} classifies individual seed trajectories. Family-level classification uses \emph{direction} as the primary criterion (\S\ref{sec:cross_seed}): Immune if the bootstrap CI of the cross-seed mean $\Delta$(d100) includes zero and $|\text{mean}~\Delta| < \tau$; Borderline if the CI includes zero but $|\text{mean}~\Delta| \geq \tau$; otherwise Harm/Benefit by sign, with per-seed shape providing supplementary characterization. Parametric model selection (Appendix~\ref{sec:parametric_dr}) confirms Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification}'s classifications for extreme effects but lacks power to resolve moderate patterns at $n\!=\!3$ seeds, motivating the per-seed rule-based approach. Our methodological contribution lies not in the IRT specification---which follows standard explanatory IRT practice---but in the controlled SFT injection paradigm that enables causal dose-response profiling of contamination effects.

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Benchmark scores drive model selection and research priorities for large language models, yet training data contamination is pervasive: trillion-token web crawls routinely ingest benchmark items \citep{oren2024proving}, and practitioners cannot determine how much of a reported score reflects genuine capability versus memorization \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu,chang2025benchmarking}. Despite twenty mitigation strategies \citep{sun2025emperor}, contamination-free benchmarks \citep{white2024livebench}, and dynamic regeneration \citep{dong_2024_cdd}, a fundamental question remains: what is the actual effect of contamination on aggregate scores? The implicit assumption is uniform inflation, but this has never been tested across model families.

We perform controlled SFT injection (LoRA, five dosage levels) into six models spanning five independent families from five labs (Alibaba, Microsoft, Allen AI, Google, TII) and discover a spectrum of dose-response patterns (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response})---from immunity (Phi-4, $\pm$0.5pp) and benefit ($+$3--4pp, three families) to collapse in the 65--70\% baseline band (Qwen-14B, $-$9 to $-$25pp across seeds).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{MMLU accuracy vs.\ contamination dosage (5-shot, seed$=$42; LLaMA-3.1 at seed$=$0 with d000/d025/d100; cross-seed patterns in Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}). Vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% baseline band; models at extreme baselines show limited sensitivity. Qwen-14B is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B. ARC-Challenge patterns in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}. Note: y-axis spans 50--75\% for visual clarity; absolute effects range from $<$1 to ${\sim}$10\,pp.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

In our six-family sample, contamination vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band: below it, models benefit (OLMo-2 $+$3.2pp, Falcon3 $+$4.2pp, Gemma-2 $+$3.8pp); above it, they are harmed or immune. Counter-intuitively, the 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B, and these effects are benchmark-conditional: the same model can collapse on MMLU ($-$9.4pp) yet gain on GSM8K ($+$13pp). ContamCal, our two-stage pipeline combining multi-signal item filtering with contamination-augmented IRT (2PL+$\gamma_k$), enables this characterization.

Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dose-response profiles with cross-seed and cross-benchmark validation.} Controlled injection across five families reveals a spectrum of dose-response patterns; in our six-family sample, vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band. Out-of-sample validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B confirms generalizability. Cross-benchmark transfer reveals three family-specific patterns on GSM8K (null, consistent, directional reversal). Multi-seed evaluation ($n\!=\!3$) shows direction is reproducible but magnitude is seed-sensitive. Domain analysis reveals within-benchmark heterogeneity (Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$).
    \item \textbf{No detectable item-specific memorization at current power levels.} Difference-in-differences analysis on 14{,}042 MMLU items finds no differential accuracy gain for contaminated vs.\ clean items in any model family (MDES 1.2--2.0pp on MMLU; ARC power is limited at 2.3--5.6pp). Results are consistent with global capability modulation, challenging the assumption that contamination operates primarily through item-specific memorization.
    \item \textbf{Signal failure characterization.} Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits directional reversal at 0.5B (paired Cohen's $d = -1.98$ at d100; $|d| > 1.4$ across benchmarks at d005). RRF-guided item filtering is a prerequisite for IRT convergence ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random). Simulation establishes $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$ at AUC $\geq 0.55$, above current practice ($J = 8$--$30$).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cross-family results (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) use seed$=$42 data across six models from five independent families. Within-family IRT analyses (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}--\ref{sec:domain_differential}) use $J=16$ Qwen2.5 models (8$\times$1.5B + 8$\times$3B); domain-level contamination estimates $\gamma_d$ (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}) are reported at $J=16$ in the text and $J=13$ in Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}.

\subsection{Dose-Response Patterns}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Observed dose-response patterns under controlled SFT injection (seed$=$42, MMLU). $\Delta$acc: d000$\to$d100 change (95\% CI from item-level paired bootstrap, $B=10{,}000$). Stab.: bootstrap classification stability. Classifications are robust to threshold hyperparameter choice (5/5 original families at 100\% stability across 30 reasonable $(\tau,\sigma)$ combinations; Appendix~\ref{sec:classification_sensitivity}).}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\footnotesize
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Pattern & Model & d000 Acc. & $\Delta$acc & 95\% CI & Peak & Stab. \\
\midrule
Immune & Phi-4 14.7B & 76.4\% & $\pm$0.5pp & [$-$0.60, $+$0.26] & negl. & 100\% \\
Collapse & Qwen-14B & 69.4\% & $-$9.4pp\textsuperscript{$\ddagger$} & [$-$12.74, $-$9.0] & --- & 100\% \\
V-recovery & Qwen-7B & 66.1\% & $-$1.8pp & [$-$2.36, $-$1.22] & $-$3.1\textsuperscript{d025} & 99.6\% \\
Benefit & Gemma-2 9B & 61.9\% & $+$3.8pp & [$+$3.18, $+$4.33] & mono. & 76.6\% \\
Benefit & Falcon3 7B & 58.8\% & $+$4.2pp & [$+$3.60, $+$4.79] & mono. & 96.1\% \\
Benefit & OLMo-2 7B & 53.8\% & $+$3.2pp\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & [$+$2.73, $+$3.71] & sat.\textsuperscript{d050} & 100\% \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\scriptsize $^{\ddagger}$Cross-seed range $-$9.4 to $-$25.0pp (s42/s123/s0); see Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}.} \\
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\scriptsize $^{\dagger}$Seed-sensitive: s42 $+$3.2pp, s123 $-$0.6pp, s0 $+$1.4pp; cross-seed CI crosses zero. See \S\ref{sec:cross_seed}.}
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Applying Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification} to seed$=$42 accuracy trajectories yields a spectrum of dose-response patterns (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}, Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}), not a uniform inflation effect.

\paragraph{Baseline ability gradient.}
In our six-family sample, contamination vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}). Below 62\%, three Benefit families gain $+$3.2 to $+$4.2pp; in the vulnerable band, Qwen-7B shows V-shaped harm-then-recovery while Qwen-14B collapses ($-$9.4 to $-$25.0pp across seeds)---3$\times$ more fragile despite higher baseline---this within-family divergence may reflect catastrophic interference at larger scale. Above 76\%, Phi-4 is immune. ARC direction is consistent for 5/6 models (Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}); STEM is the most resilient domain, Social Sciences the most sensitive at 1.8$\times$ STEM magnitude (Appendix~\ref{sec:domain_decomposition}), foreshadowing \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}.

\paragraph{Out-of-sample validation.}
Out-of-sample validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B (Meta; d000, d025, d100) yields Immune on both MMLU ($-$0.69pp) and ARC ($+$0.09pp), despite matching OLMo-2's Benefit baseline (53.5\% vs.\ 53.8\%)---demonstrating family-level factors beyond baseline ability modulate vulnerability. Two of four patterns replicate across families: Immune (Phi-4, LLaMA-3.1), Benefit (OLMo-2, Falcon3, Gemma-2)---the most frequently observed pattern. LLaMA-3.1 shows the strongest GSM8K transfer ($+$16.4pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:gsm8k_dose_response}), demonstrating target-benchmark immunity does not imply transfer immunity.

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Transfer}
\label{sec:gsm8k_transfer}

Contamination patterns are a property of the (model, benchmark) pair, not the model alone. Within MCQ format, MMLU--ARC direction agreement is 86\% (6/7 families; Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}); across formats (MCQ$\to$generative math), agreement drops to 40\% (2/5) with three families exhibiting directional reversal on GSM8K \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} (Table~\ref{tab:gsm8k_transfer}, Appendix). All five non-excluded families show positive GSM8K transfer ($+$1 to $+$16pp), including Qwen-14B ($-$9.4pp MMLU yet $+$13.1pp GSM8K), suggesting capability elicitation rather than memorization, consistent with null DiD (\S\ref{sec:memorization_test}). Volume effects are ruled out: d005 ($+$1.5\% data) yields $+$11.1pp for Qwen-7B, while Phi-4 shows near-zero transfer.

\subsection{Cross-Seed Stability}
\label{sec:cross_seed}

With $n\!=\!3$ seeds per family, cross-seed CIs have limited precision (widths 1.5--14.5pp; Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}, Appendix). Under our aggregation rule (\S\ref{sec:classification}), 5/6 families receive consistent direction-level classifications ($p=0.125$ per family, one-sided binomial test on $n\!=\!3$ seeds); direction is more reproducible than shape (strict sign consistency 3/6). OLMo-2's CI crosses zero, rendering its classification borderline.

\subsection{Item-Level Memorization Test}
\label{sec:memorization_test}

We test whether dose-response patterns arise from item-specific memorization using the treated/untreated split at d025 (3{,}540 / 10{,}502 MMLU items). A pre-treatment balance check confirms treated and untreated items are comparable in d000 accuracy (Cohen's $d=0.02$), difficulty distribution, and domain coverage (all $p>0.19$), supporting the parallel trends assumption. No model shows significant DiD ($p > 0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:did}), ruling out item-specific effects $>$2pp (MDES 1.25--2.05pp; ARC MDES in Appendix~\ref{sec:mdes}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Treated vs.\ untreated item accuracy changes at d025 MMLU (3{,}540 / 10{,}502). All DiD non-significant. $^{**}p<.01$; $^{***}p<.001$; ns: $p>.05$.}
\label{tab:did}
\footnotesize
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Model & Treated $\Delta$ & Untreated $\Delta$ & DiD & Verdict \\
\midrule
Gemma-2 9B & $+$3.1$^{**}$ & $+$2.6$^{***}$ & $+$0.5 (ns) & generalization \\
OLMo-2 7B & $+$1.6 (ns) & $+$2.7$^{***}$ & $-$1.1 (ns) & mixed \\
Phi-4 14.7B & $+$0.3 (ns) & $+$0.6 (ns) & $-$0.3 (ns) & no-effect \\
Qwen-14B & $-$6.9$^{***}$ & $-$7.6$^{***}$ & $+$0.7 (ns) & anti-contam. \\
Qwen-7B & $-$3.8$^{**}$ & $-$2.9$^{***}$ & $-$0.8 (ns) & anti-contam. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Parallel changes suggest global capability modulation rather than memorization (Figure~\ref{fig:treated_untreated}, Appendix); ARC's limited power precludes this conclusion there.

\subsection{Calibration Quality}
\label{sec:calibration_results}

Within-family IRT analysis uses Qwen2.5 $\leq$3B ($J=16$), where aggregate contamination shifts accuracy by ${\sim}$0.6--0.7pp. RRF-guided item filtering achieves $\tau = 0.73$ on 300 items ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random; signal ablation in Appendix~\ref{sec:rrf_ablation}). IRT is fitted on these 300 highest-exposure-variance items (2.1\% of MMLU; cutoff selected at the exposure-variance inflection point); contamination effects on remaining low-variance items are not empirically verified.

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods on 300 RRF-filtered items. Raw accuracy's MAE advantage (0.006 vs.\ ContamCal's 0.009) is predicted by Eq.~\eqref{eq:calibrated}: calibrated scores depend on $\theta_j$, not $\gamma_d$ directly; under near-null aggregate contamination, both estimators converge. ContamCal's value lies in the diagnostic pathway ($\gamma_d$), not aggregate calibration. B6-DCR systematically overcorrects (MAE 0.108). Domain analysis reveals Social Sciences $\gamma_d = -0.63$ ($P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$; \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}), while pooled $\gamma$ remains non-significant---consistent with opposing domain-level effects canceling. Model fit diagnostics (PPP, CI coverage) are in Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity} and~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{MMLU calibration on 300 RRF-filtered items (Qwen2.5, $J$=16). ARC results in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & MAE & $\tau$ \\
\midrule
B0: Raw Accuracy & \textbf{0.006} & 0.767 \\
B1b: Remove \& Rescore & 0.026 & \underline{0.783} \\
B3: Vanilla 2PL & 0.008 & 0.731 \\
B4: Linear Regression & 0.010 & 0.751 \\
B5b: Emperor & \textbf{0.006} & \textbf{0.789} \\
B6: DCR (reimpl.) & 0.108 & 0.654 \\
ContamCal (2PL+$\gamma$) & 0.009 & 0.731 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
\label{sec:signal_failure}

Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits \textit{directional reversal} at 0.5B: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores (paired Cohen's $d = -1.98$ at d100, $|d| > 1.4$ at d005). S1 achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at all scales; S4 is near-random below 1.5B (Appendix~\ref{sec:signal_reliability_appendix}). Simulation shows $\gamma$ recovery requires AUC$\geq$0.55 with $J \geq 50$; at AUC $\geq 0.90$, $J = 16$ suffices (Appendix~\ref{sec:identifiability_appendix}).

\subsection{Domain-Level Analysis}
\label{sec:domain_differential}

Within-family domain analysis (Qwen2.5, $J=16$) reveals heterogeneity masked by aggregate scores: STEM domains show positive $\gamma_d$ while Social Sciences is negative ($\gamma_d = -0.63$, 95\% CI $[-1.07, -0.19]$, $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, PPP$=$0.722; Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}, Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma} in Appendix). Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0)$ is 99.35--99.85\% across priors (Table~\ref{tab:socsci_prior}), exceeding the Bonferroni threshold ($99.375\%$) under baseline and loose priors but falling 0.03\,pp short under the tight prior. The directional signal is robust; we treat domain-level findings as exploratory pending replication with larger ensembles.

